pra: 0164 - consolidate 0148+0162+0163 into one patch generated against CI's actual tree

The 0162/0162b floods were never the whole story. CI's kernel object
proved the shipped driver was the PRE-0148 state: "CMD18 unavailable
(%d)" (0142-era string), no stop_transfer (0x0cdb immediates: zero),
no ladder. Root cause: patch 0148 has been silently skipped by CI
since the day it landed ("error: patch failed:
bsd/dev/pra_sdhci.c:395" in every run log, swallowed by build.sh's
skip-on-fail guard), and 0162/0163 were authored against the
0148-present lineage, so they failed too. The phone therefore ran
0142's unfixed CMD18 (no CMD12 abort, no per-block buffer-ready clear)
the moment 0162 dropped emmcmulti=0 from the cmdline - hence the
"card busy, skip read" flood. The emmcmulti=2 "manual stop" diagnostic
never existed in any shipped kernel; that experiment is void.

Every boot before 0162 was masked by emmcmulti=0 (per-sector CMD17 at
runtime), which is why the skip stayed invisible for weeks.

0164 replaces 0148+0162+0163 (all removed) with ONE patch generated
against the driver file CI actually builds (reconstructed
deterministically from pristine xnu-8796.141.3 + the driver-lineage
patches 0139,0140,0142,0144,0146,0149,0151,0156,0158,0159,0161 - the
sequence CI's log shows applied). Verified: lineage + 0164 is
byte-identical to the intended final driver (per-sector CMD17 default,
CMD23+CMD18 opt-in via emmcmulti=1, recover-and-latch after any chunk
failure, rate-limited busy prints). The workflow now asserts the eMMC
markers (CMD23 lba=, busy-skip) so a silent skip can never happen
again.

Local-verification trap worth remembering: git 2.56 skip-detects
already-applied patches (rc=0, "Skipped patch"), so local
`git apply --check` can validate a patch CI will reject; and GNU patch
treats the fabricated `index 0000000..1111111` lines as new-file
markers. Verify with GNU patch --fuzz=0 on index-stripped diffs.

